<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heng Samrin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">b. 1934</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heng Samrin was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> commander in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>'s Eastern Zone who fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> in 1978 to escape the purges. After the Vietnamese invasion of January 1979 he became head of state of the People's Republic of Kampuchea and leader of its ruling party, though real influence passed increasingly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hun-sen">Hun Sen</a>. He remained a senior figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodian-peoples-party">Cambodian People's Party</a> and later served for many years as president of the National Assembly.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heng-samrin.jpg" alt="Heng Samrin" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hul Reaksmey/VOA Khmer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heng_Samrin_2019.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
